[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"jQuery Setup and Basic Syntax"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"jQuery is a fast, small, and feature-rich JavaScript library. It simplifies HTML document traversing, event handling, animation, and Ajax interactions for rapid web development. This tutorial covers how to include jQuery in your project and introduces basic syntax."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Including jQuery in Your Project"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are two primary ways to include jQuery in your project: using a Content Delivery Network (CDN) or hosting the jQuery library locally."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Using a CDN"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A CDN hosts jQuery on servers around the world, allowing users to download it quickly. This is often the easiest and fastest way to include jQuery."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"<!-- Include jQuery from a CDN -->
<script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js"></script>"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Place this &lt;script&gt; tag within the &lt;head&gt; or before the closing &lt;body&gt; tag of your HTML document.  It's generally recommended to place scripts before the closing &lt;body&gt; tag for faster page loading."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can find the latest jQuery CDN links on the official jQuery website or from other CDN providers like cdnjs.com."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Using a Local File"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also download the jQuery library and host it on your own server. This gives you more control over the version of jQuery used and can be useful for offline development."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Download the latest version of jQuery from jquery.com."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Place the downloaded file (e.g., jquery-3.7.1.min.js) in a directory within your project (e.g., 'js')."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Include the file in your HTML document using a &lt;script&gt; tag."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<!-- Include jQuery from a local file -->
<script src="js/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"></script>"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic jQuery Syntax"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"jQuery syntax is designed to make it easy to select HTML elements and perform actions on them. The basic syntax is:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"$(selector).action()"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"$ is the jQuery alias for the jQuery object."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"selector is used to find HTML elements."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"action() is the method to be performed on the selected element(s)."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Hiding a Paragraph"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"To hide all paragraph elements on a page, you would use the following code:"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"$("p").hide();"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code selects all &lt;p&gt; elements and then calls the 'hide()' method to hide them."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Document Ready Function"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"To ensure that your jQuery code runs after the DOM (Document Object Model) is fully loaded, you should wrap it in a document ready function:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"$(document).ready(function() {
  // jQuery code here
});

//Shorthand version
$(function() {
  // jQuery code here
});"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"This ensures that your jQuery code will only execute once the page's DOM is ready."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Click Event"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attaching a click event to a button:"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"$("#myButton").click(function() {
  alert("Button was clicked!");
});"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code selects the element with the ID 'myButton' and attaches a click event handler that displays an alert when the button is clicked."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"By including jQuery in your project, either through a CDN or a local file, and understanding the basic syntax, you can start leveraging its powerful features to simplify your web development tasks. Remember to always wrap your jQuery code inside the $(document).ready() function to ensure it runs after the DOM is fully loaded."}]
